Logic :


I solved this problem using backtracking because, for every digit, I have multiple letter choices, and I need to generate every possible combination.

I first create a mapping from digits to their corresponding letters. Then I recursively process the input string one digit at a time.

At each recursive call, I look at the current digit and get all the letters mapped to it. I then iterate through those letters.

For each letter:

I choose the letter by appending it to the current StringBuilder.
I explore by recursively processing the next digit.
Once the recursive call returns, I undo my choice by removing the last character from the StringBuilder. This is the backtracking step, which allows me to try the next possible letter.

When index reaches the length of the input string, it means I have selected one letter for every digit. At that point, the current StringBuilder contains one complete combination, so I convert it into a string and store it in the answer list.

So the overall pattern is:

Choose a letter → Explore the next digit → Undo the choice → Try the next letter.



Code :

class Solution {
    public List<String> letterCombinations(String digits) {
        String[] arr={"abc","def","ghi","jkl","mno","pqrs","tuv","wxyz"};
        return lettercomb(arr,digits,0,new StringBuilder(),new ArrayList<>());
    }
    List<String> lettercomb(String[] arr,String digits,int index,StringBuilder str,List<String> ans){
        if(index==digits.length()){
            ans.add(str.toString());
            return ans;
        }
        String s=arr[(digits.charAt(index)-'0')-2];
        for(int i=0;i<s.length();i++){
            str.append(s.charAt(i));
            ans=lettercomb(arr,digits,index+1,str,ans);
            str.deleteCharAt(str.length()-1);
        }
        return ans;
    }
}
